## 1. <u>Reverse a String Without Using a Built-in Reverse Method</u>

### 🎯 Concept
Reverse a string by reading its characters from the last index to the first index.

### 💻 Code
```java
package p4;

public class ReverseAMethodWithoutUsingBuiltInVerse {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String word = "hello";
        String reverse = "";

        for (int i = word.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            reverse = reverse + word.charAt(i);
        }

        System.out.println("Reverse String = " + reverse);
    }
}
```
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

### 📤 Output
```text
Reverse String = olleh
```
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

### 🔍 Explanation
- `word.length() - 1` gives the last index.
- `i--` moves backwards.
- `word.charAt(i)` gets the current character.
- `reverse = reverse + word.charAt(i)` adds the character to the result.

### 🧪 Dry Run

| Index | Character | Reverse |
|---:|:---:|---|
| 4 | o | o |
| 3 | l | ol |
| 2 | l | oll |
| 1 | e | olle |
| 0 | h | olleh |

### ⏱️ Complexity
- Time: O(n²) with repeated string concatenation in a loop.
- Extra space: O(n).

### 🧠 Memory Trick
Start from the **last index** and move towards index `0`.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 2. <u>Count Vowels in a String</u>

### 🎯 Concept
Count the vowels `a`, `e`, `i`, `o`, and `u`.

### 💻 Code
```java
package p4;

public class CountVowelsInAString {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String word = "education";
        int count = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) {
            char ch = Character.toLowerCase(word.charAt(i));

            if (ch == 'a' || ch == 'e' || ch == 'i'
                    || ch == 'o' || ch == 'u') {
                count++;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Count of Vowels = " + count);
    }
}
```
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

### 📤 Output
```text
Count of Vowels = 5
```
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

### 🔍 Explanation
- Visit each character using a `for` loop.
- `Character.toLowerCase()` allows uppercase vowels to be counted too.
- `||` means OR: if any vowel condition matches, increase `count`.

### ⏱️ Complexity
- Time: O(n)
- Extra space: O(1), excluding implementation details of string operations.

### 🧠 Memory Trick
Vowels are **A, E, I, O, U**.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 3. <u>Count Consonants in a String</u>

### 🎯 Concept
Count English letters that are not vowels. Ignore spaces, digits, and special characters.

### 💻 Code
```java
package p4;

public class CountConsonants {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String word = "hello world";
        int count = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) {
            char ch = Character.toLowerCase(word.charAt(i));

            if (Character.isLetter(ch)
                    && ch != 'a' && ch != 'e'
                    && ch != 'i' && ch != 'o'
                    && ch != 'u') {
                count++;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Count of Consonants = " + count);
    }
}
```
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

### 📤 Output
```text
Count of Consonants = 7
```
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

### 🔍 Explanation
- `Character.isLetter(ch)` checks whether the character is a letter.
- The remaining conditions exclude vowels.
- `&&` means all conditions must be true.

### 🧠 Important Point
Checking only that a character is not a vowel is not enough. A space or digit is also not a vowel, but it is not a consonant either.

### ⏱️ Complexity
- Time: O(n)
- Extra space: O(1).

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 4. <u>Count Digits in a String</u>

### 🎯 Concept
Count numeric characters from `0` to `9` inside a string.

### 💻 Code
```java
package p4;

public class CountDigitsInAString {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = "java123 is fun45";
        int count = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < str.length(); i++) {
            char ch = str.charAt(i);

            if (ch >= '0' && ch <= '9') {
                count++;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Digits Count = " + count);
    }
}
```
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

### 📤 Output
```text
Digits Count = 5
```
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

### 🔍 Explanation
- `ch >= '0'` checks that the character is at least `0`.
- `ch <= '9'` checks that it is at most `9`.
- Both conditions together identify digit characters.

### ⏱️ Complexity
- Time: O(n)
- Extra space: O(1).

### 🧠 Memory Trick
For a character range, use **single quotes**: `'0'` and `'9'`.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

